{"id":"overtime-police-against-peers","tier":"REDFLAG","title":"Police overtime: San Francisco against other large California employers","question":"Is overtime in San Francisco’s police high against the same kind of department elsewhere in California?","finding":"In 2025, San Francisco’s police overtime was 37.7% of regular pay (50,098 dollars per paid position), ranking 1 of 7; the middle of the others was 28.4% (31,292 dollars per position). Highest: San Francisco, Long Beach (city), Oakland (city). In 2019 San Francisco’s share was 18.2%, rank 4 of 7 (middle of the others 19.0%). 193 of its positions were paid more in overtime than in regular pay (70.93 per 1,000 salaried positions).","how":"State Controller raw export. Departments are picked by the name each employer reports (a department name containing “police”, leaving out accountability, commission, auditor and review bodies). Only employer-years with 50 or more paid positions in such departments are shown. Overtime share is overtime divided by regular pay. The export does not say whether a year is a calendar or a fiscal year. San Francisco’s overtime here is $480.3 million for 2024; the City’s own payroll file has $468.9 million for fiscal 2024 and $471.0 million for fiscal 2025, so the two sources are close but not the same and are not mixed in any one figure.","limits":"Department names differ between employers, so a department can be missed or civilian staff counted in. Overtime depends on vacancies, minimum staffing rules set in labor agreements, and paid work for outside events billed to others; a high share is a question about staffing, not proof of abuse. A position is a row, not a person.","columns":[{"k":"employer","t":"Employer","type":"org"},{"k":"year","t":"Year","type":"year"},{"k":"positions","t":"Paid positions","type":"int"},{"k":"otShare","t":"Overtime as share of regular pay","type":"pct"},{"k":"otPer","t":"Overtime per paid position","type":"usd"},{"k":"otherShare","t":"Other pay as shar